---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

const contact = 'mailto:eliheuer@gmail.com?subject=Font%20Garden';
---

<Layout title="Font Garden — Fonts that are neural networks">
  <main class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 pb-24 pt-10 md:pt-16">
    <header class="mb-20 md:mb-28">
      <a href="/" class="text-lg tracking-tight hover:text-accent">Font Garden</a>
    </header>

    <!-- Hero -->
    <section class="mb-20 md:mb-28">
      <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-6xl leading-[1.05] tracking-tight">
        We are building fonts that are neural networks.
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-8 text-xl md:text-2xl leading-snug text-neutral-500">
        For Arabic. For Nastaʿliq. For Latin. For the scripts and styles that
        OpenType never served.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Problem -->
    <section class="mb-16 space-y-5 text-lg md:text-xl leading-relaxed">
      <p>
        An OpenType font is a box of fixed shapes: one outline per glyph,
        reused everywhere, in cells that digital type inherited from metal
        sorts. Latin fits this system. Much of the written world does not.
      </p>
      <p>
        Naskh, and above all Nastaʿliq, is one continuous, context-dependent
        stroke. The OpenType workaround is thousands of glyphs and
        substitution rules, and the result has never matched the manuscripts
        it comes from.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Format -->
    <section class="mb-16 space-y-5 text-lg md:text-xl leading-relaxed">
      <h2 class="text-sm uppercase tracking-widest text-neutral-500">
        The NeuralType format
      </h2>
      <p>
        Our answer is NeuralType, the <code>.ntf</code> font format. A
        <code>.ntf</code> file is a small neural network. The character, its
        joining form, and settings such as elongation go into the network. The
        letterform comes out and is traced into vector outlines at the moment
        you type it. The shapes are computed, not retrieved.
      </p>
      <p>
        Computed does not mean random. The same text at the same settings
        produces identical outlines on every machine. Learned axes such as
        weight and elongation are continuous numbers: variable fonts without
        masters. The file is a short header and the network weights. The
        weights are the font.
      </p>
      <p>
        The spec and the Rust rendering engine are open source under
        Apache-2.0 or MIT at
        <a href="https://github.com/eliheuer/post-opentype" class="underline hover:text-accent"
          >github.com/eliheuer/post-opentype</a
        >. Live demos, with real editable text set by neural fonts, are at
        <a href="https://elih.net/blog/neuraltype" class="underline hover:text-accent"
          >elih.net/blog/neuraltype</a
        >.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- How we work -->
    <section class="mb-16 space-y-5 text-lg md:text-xl leading-relaxed">
      <h2 class="text-sm uppercase tracking-widest text-neutral-500">
        How we work
      </h2>
      <p>
        These fonts are grown more than they are drawn. A teacher describes
        the letterforms, a small model learns them, and the trained model
        becomes the font. We tend our fonts after release: retrain them,
        extend them to new scripts, and refine them.
      </p>
      <p>
        Our work continues the international typographic style, extended to
        the scripts that modernism ignored, with a focus on the Perso-Arabic
        script. Every font is open. Every model ships with open weights. The
        training data and the specs are published.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Plan -->
    <section class="mb-16 text-lg md:text-xl leading-relaxed">
      <h2 class="text-sm uppercase tracking-widest text-neutral-500">
        The plan
      </h2>
      <ol class="mt-5 space-y-8">
        <li>
          <h3 class="font-bold">1. Define the format</h3>
          <p class="mt-2">
            Publish the <code>.ntf</code> spec, the rendering engine, and live
            demos. This work is underway and open source.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3 class="font-bold">2. Release fonts and models</h3>
          <p class="mt-2">
            A first catalog of <code>.ntf</code> fonts and open-weight models,
            free under open licenses. Coming soon.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3 class="font-bold">3. Build the platform</h3>
          <p class="mt-2">
            Distribution for <code>.ntf</code> fonts, hosted APIs for our
            models, and tools to train and tend your own. This is the
            business.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <!-- Who -->
    <section class="mb-16 space-y-5 text-lg md:text-xl leading-relaxed">
      <h2 class="text-sm uppercase tracking-widest text-neutral-500">Who</h2>
      <p>
        Font Garden is founded by
        <a href="https://elih.net" class="underline hover:text-accent">Eli Heuer</a>,
        a type designer and font engineer. The research notes and demos behind
        the format are on
        <a href="https://elih.net" class="underline hover:text-accent">elih.net</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Contact -->
    <section class="mb-24 text-lg md:text-xl leading-relaxed">
      <h2 class="text-sm uppercase tracking-widest text-neutral-500">
        Stay in touch
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-5">
        We will announce the first fonts and models by email.
        <a href={contact} class="underline hover:text-accent">Write to us</a>
        to get the announcement, or to talk about the format, the models, or
        the seed round.
      </p>
    </section>

    <footer class="border-t border-black pt-4 text-base text-neutral-500">
      <p>
        Font Garden ·
        <a href="https://github.com/fontgarden" class="hover:text-accent">GitHub</a> ·
        <a href={contact} class="hover:text-accent">Contact</a>
      </p>
    </footer>
  </main>
</Layout>
